{
"dur": 117.118,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 4.687,
"text": "First, where was this website? Not anywhere you could find on Google.",
"end": 4.962
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 4.962,
"vo_start": 5.237,
"vo_end": 12.077,
"text": "Picture the internet as an iceberg. The tip is the part search engines can see: news sites, shops, videos.",
"end": 12.352
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 12.352,
"vo_start": 12.627,
"vo_end": 20.707,
"text": "Below the water is the deep web. It sounds scary, but it's mostly your own stuff: email, bank accounts, medical records, anything behind a password.",
"end": 20.982
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 20.982,
"vo_start": 21.257,
"vo_end": 29.297,
"text": "You'll often hear that this hidden part is ninety-six percent of the internet. Nobody can really measure that. But it is far bigger than the tip.",
"end": 29.572
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 29.572,
"vo_start": 29.847,
"vo_end": 36.007,
"text": "And right at the bottom is a small, dark corner: the dark web. Sites you can only reach with special software.",
"end": 36.282
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 36.282,
"vo_start": 36.557,
"vo_end": 42.357,
"text": "Normally, every time you visit a website, your connection carries an IP address. It can point back to you.",
"end": 42.632
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 42.632,
"vo_start": 42.907,
"vo_end": 48.067,
"text": "The software that hides it is called Tor. It stands for The Onion Router.",
"end": 48.342
},
{
"i": 8,
"start": 48.342,
"vo_start": 48.617,
"vo_end": 53.777,
"text": "Your request is wrapped in three layers of encryption, then bounced through three volunteer computers called relays.",
"end": 54.052
},
{
"i": 9,
"start": 54.052,
"vo_start": 54.327,
"vo_end": 63.087,
"text": "Each relay peels off one layer. The first knows who you are, but not where you're going. The last knows where you're going, but not who you are.",
"end": 63.362
},
{
"i": 10,
"start": 63.362,
"vo_start": 63.637,
"vo_end": 70.516,
"text": "And here's the surprise. Tor was first developed at the US Naval Research Laboratory, to protect government communications.",
"end": 70.791
},
{
"i": 11,
"start": 70.791,
"vo_start": 71.066,
"vo_end": 80.186,
"text": "Today it's used by journalists, activists and ordinary people who want privacy. Using Tor is legal in most countries. Buying illegal goods through it is not.",
"end": 80.461
},
{
"i": 12,
"start": 80.461,
"vo_start": 80.736,
"vo_end": 86.496,
"text": "So Tor could hide who you were. But it left one big problem. How do you pay?",
"end": 86.771
},
{
"i": 13,
"start": 86.771,
"vo_start": 87.046,
"vo_end": 91.81,
"text": "You can't buy something illegal with a credit card. The bank keeps a record, and your name is on it.",
"end": 92.085
},
{
"i": 14,
"start": 92.085,
"vo_start": 92.36,
"vo_end": 97.118,
"text": "In January 2009, someone using the name Satoshi Nakamoto launched Bitcoin.",
"end": 97.393
},
{
"i": 15,
"start": 97.393,
"vo_start": 97.668,
"vo_end": 103.668,
"text": "It's digital money that moves from one online wallet to another, with no bank in the middle and no name on the account.",
"end": 103.943
},
{
"i": 16,
"start": 103.943,
"vo_start": 104.218,
"vo_end": 112.898,
"text": "But it isn't invisible. Every payment is written on a public record that anyone can read. The wallets just have codes instead of names.",
"end": 113.173
},
{
"i": 17,
"start": 113.173,
"vo_start": 113.448,
"vo_end": 116.168,
"text": "Remember that. It matters later.",
"end": 117.118
}
],
